I’ll first inspect the measurement metadata, pattern format, and the reusable analysis utilities, then build a candidate-phase inventory and establish a GSAS-II baseline before refining alternatives. I’ll keep the structural and peak-shape evidence tied to the saved project and validate every submitted artifact by reopening it.

The chemistry points first to lead tungstates, but the 500 °C Pb₃O₄/WO₃ route can retain precursors or form non-1:1 lead-rich intermediates. I’m checking the measured peak inventory and the installed indexing/database/refinement interfaces before assigning any phase.

A coherent scheelite PbWO₄ family is already visible: its predicted peaks align after a roughly +0.4° low-angle specimen displacement, including the high-angle sequence. Several strong peaks remain outside that family, so I’m treating them as a real second-phase signal and screening precursor polymorphs and charge-balanced Pb–W–O alternatives under the same angular correction.

The residual inventory is chemically and crystallographically consistent with retained room-temperature monoclinic WO₃, not a lead-rich tungstate: its strongest unique peaks at 23.53, 24.02, 24.84, 33.66, 34.60, and 42.27° all match one common displacement correction. Pb₃O₄ remains on the watch list because several weaker lines coincide, and I’ll test it quantitatively after the two-phase baseline converges.

The Pb₃O₄ watch-list challenge is decisive: adding room-temperature tetragonal minium lowers Rwp from 10.58% to 8.02% and explains its unique 26.81, 31.22, 32.55, 50.38, 52.52, and 58.54° families. The provisional crystalline fractions are about 60% PbWO₄, 26% WO₃, and 14% Pb₃O₄; I’m now comparing physical source/polymorph alternatives and then testing width and intensity corrections one at a time.

The nuisance tests separate cleanly: polarization is strongly supported (Rwp 7.92→6.30%, refined 0.997), while roughness drives a parameter to its bound with 99.99% correlation and is rejected. Extra U or W also hits zero and will be pruned. I’m fixing the effectively full polarization at 1.0, keeping an eight-term smooth background, and replaying size-versus-strain phase by phase from that saved baseline.

The source inventory now converges on scheelite PbWO₄ + residual monoclinic WO₃ + Pb₃O₄. I’m doing the last model-discrimination pass—texture and directional broadening from the saved isotropic baseline—then I’ll freeze, reopen, audit peak widths, and export the scorer-ready files.

The physical challenges are behaving cleanly: axial/diagonal texture changes Rwp by only about 0.00–0.01 percentage point and the March ratios stay near 1, so texture is unsupported. The first uniaxial WO₃ strain model is likewise indistinguishable from isotropic; I’m finishing the other symmetry directions before locking the simpler model.

The watch-list replay found a real fourth phase: orthorhombic massicot PbO. Its nonnegative scale is ~60σ from zero, it accounts for the coherent 29–32° residual family, and Rwp drops from 4.57% to 3.74%; tetragonal litharge and PbO₂ do not. I’ll now reconverge the four-phase model and repeat the nuisance/profile and peak-shape audits, as required after an inventory change.

With massicot included, the clean common profile now supports U+X (W remains at its nonnegative bound). I’m restarting each phase’s size/strain test from that saved U+X baseline; this matters because the X-only WO₃ strain solution went negative and is therefore physically rejected.

The combined, physically preferred PbWO₄/Pb₃O₄ size model is replay-stable at Rwp 3.437% and GOF 1.385; fractions are currently about 59.0/20.8/17.3/2.83 wt% (PbWO₄/WO₃/Pb₃O₄/PbO). I’m now testing whether any additional strain term is complementary, or merely substitutes for U/X or size, before pruning the final model.

The two massicot broadening models are both replay-stable: a ~55 nm size model gives Rwp 3.191%, while an extreme ~1.0% strain model gives 3.117%. Because that strain may be compensating for the average 1961 structure, I’m comparing the later modulated orthorhombic PbO structure under identical nuisance terms before deciding which fraction is defensible.

The modulated PbO record is computationally heavier because of its 32-formula-unit supercell. Its neutral replay is already clearly inferior (Rwp 3.96% versus 3.44% for the average massicot model) and requires an implausible ~2% contraction of the long axis; I’m allowing the matched size-broadening replay to finish so the rejection is source-model rather than profile-model dependent.

The measured-width audit resolves the massicot ambiguity: the 53 nm size model tracks its low/mid/high FWHM (0.258/0.342/0.465° calculated versus 0.263/0.282/0.406° observed) better than the extreme-strain model (0.250/0.409/0.580°), even though the latter lowers global Rwp. I’m retaining the physical size model and now checking directional size and final four-phase texture terms against it.

The four-phase model is now stable. I’m finishing the last directional-broadening and texture challenges, then I’ll replay the watch list against that exact model and freeze the scorer-ready project and exports.

The final challenges found two modest but reproducible effects: a diagonal massicot texture term and directional broadening in Pb₃O₄. I’m testing them together because they affect intensity and width independently; a WO₃ strain trial was rejected after forcing a profile coefficient out of bounds.

The combined replay supports Pb₃O₄ directional size broadening, while its texture term collapses to unity; massicot’s extra size anisotropy is too small and fraction-sensitive to retain. I’m therefore using the lean model (one Pb₃O₄ anisotropy plus one replay-stable massicot texture term) for the final phase watch-list challenge.

A second WO₃ form is the only watch-list candidate with substantial support: triclinic WO₃ refines to about 1.4 wt% and lowers Rwp from 3.110% to 3.082%. Because it is closely related to monoclinic WO₃, I’m checking refined-cell stability and phase-specific widths before deciding whether this is a real polymorph or merely a surrogate for WO₃ line-shape error.

The triclinic component survives the harder test: its literature cell refines only modestly, its fraction becomes 2.61(12) wt%, no parameter is bound, and the scale correlation with monoclinic WO₃ is moderate (−0.65), not singular. Size/strain additions inflate that fraction and correlate strongly, so I’m retaining only the refined cell and now replaying the weak-phase watch list once more against the five-phase model.

The five-phase replay remains numerically well behaved. The second watch-list pass is now testing whether reduced tungsten oxides or another WO₃ polymorph still explain any residual once triclinic WO₃ is present; zero-scale candidates are being discarded automatically.

The final five-phase model is selected. I’m now extending the replay to every measured point (9.903–100.095°), explicitly pruning all rejected terms, and generating the CIFs, profile, JSON, and reopened-project consistency checks from that single saved project.

The full-range fit is visually strong and the export validation passes, but one structured high-angle residual family remains. I’m doing one last compatible anisotropy challenge on the dominant scheelite phase; if it is not replay-stable and materially better, the current lean project remains final.

The scheelite challenge supports a mild [001] size anisotropy (about 61 nm transverse versus 57 nm axial): it is replay-stable, adds one parameter, lowers χ² by ~29, and has no bound or high new correlation. Both anisotropic-strain trials failed at bounds, so I’m accepting only this size term and regenerating the final artifacts once.

The final watch-list replay confirms the stopping point: orthorhombic WO₃ and W₁₈O₄₉ each absorb only ~0.3–0.35 wt% and give similar tiny improvements, so the residual is not phase-specific. I’m leaving that sub-percent signal unassigned and documenting it as an identification limit rather than adding a non-unique sixth phase.

Completed the five-phase refinement:

- PbWO₄ scheelite: 58.50 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 17.73 wt%
- Pb₃O₄: 17.03 wt%
- Massicot PbO: 4.16 wt%
- Triclinic WO₃: 2.58 wt% (tentative)

Combined WO₃ is 20.31 wt%. The full-range fit gives Rwp = 3.026%, GOF = 1.221. The triclinic/monoclinic split is overlap-limited; a further ~0.3–0.4 wt% residual phase signal was non-unique and not assigned.

